{"recall":{"defs":[["Decision tree","Hierarchy of attribute tests ending in class leaves."],["Entropy","Expected information; impurity of a class distribution."],["Information gain","Entropy reduction from a split."],["Gini index","Probability two random draws differ in class."],["Gain ratio","Gain normalised by split information."],["MDL","Prefer the hypothesis with the shortest total description."]],"formulas":["H(S)=-\\sum_ip_i\\log_2p_i","\\text{Gain}(S,A)=H(S)-\\sum_v\\tfrac{|S_v|}{|S|}H(S_v)","\\text{Gini}(t)=1-\\sum_ip_i(t)^2","\\text{GainRatio}=\\tfrac{\\text{Gain}(S,A)}{-\\sum_i\\frac{|S_i|}{|S|}\\log_2\\frac{|S_i|}{|S|}}","h_{MDL}=\\arg\\min_hL_{C_1}(h)+L_{C_2}(D\\mid h)"],"takeaways":["Trees encode OR-of-AND rules and are easy to read.","ID3 greedily maximises information gain; Gini is a cheaper impurity.","Continuous attributes become thresholds at class-change midpoints.","Many-valued attributes need gain ratio or binary splits.","Control overfitting with pre-pruning, post-pruning on a prune set, or MDL."],"confused":[["Entropy vs information gain","Impurity of one set vs reduction from a split."],["Gain vs gain ratio","Raw reduction vs reduction per bit of split information."],["Pre- vs post-pruning","Before vs after full growth."],["Gini index vs Gini coefficient","Impurity measure vs inequality measure (different)."]],"minute":"A decision tree is a set of if-then rules learned greedily. Entropy H = −Σ p log₂ p measures how mixed a node is; information gain is the drop in weighted entropy after a split, and ID3 picks the attribute with the largest gain, recursing until leaves are pure. On PlayTennis, Outlook (0.246) is the root, then Humidity under Sunny and Wind under Rain. Continuous attributes use the best midpoint threshold; Gini 1 − Σ p² is an alternative impurity; gain ratio corrects the bias toward many-valued attributes. Trees overfit, so limit growth, prune with a validation set, or use MDL to balance tree size against errors."},"flashcards":